# A struct with no `__call__` member, returned as the final value of a zero-argument
# closure, must behave exactly like every other value type when that closure is
# invoked as a bare statement (`$(f)`) -- the struct is the *result*, not something
# to be called further. Found while building a pty test helper that wraps `pty:
# create` (whose own result is exactly such a struct) in a closure: `eval_struct` in
# src/lang/command_invocation.rs unconditionally required a `__call__` member,
# even with zero arguments, instead of falling through to the generic "forward this
# value" behavior every other type gets in that same zero-argument case (see
# `eval_command_definition`'s final catch-all arm).
$f := {|| $($struct.of x=1 y=2)}
$r := $(f)
assert (($r.x) == 1) "closure's returned struct should be usable after the call"
